<header><p class="st-kicker">khanate · Central Asia and the Steppe</p><h1 class="st-title">Turpan Khanate</h1><p class="st-meta">1462 CE – 1680 CE (approx.) · capital Turpan</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The eastern successor of the Chagatai Khanate, ruling Turpan and Hami, where Islam finally displaced the region's old Buddhist culture.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/moghulistan">Moghulistan</a> divided in the 1460s, its eastern lands around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turfan">Turpan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aksu">Aksu</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-kucha">Kucha</a> formed a separate khanate. From 1487 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunus-khan">Yunus Khan's</a> son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-alaq">Sultan Ahmad Khan</a> ruled it, and Turpan became the capital of what is sometimes called Eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moghulistan">Moghulistan</a> or Uyghurstan.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turfan">Turpan</a> khans ruled the oases of Turpan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karashahr">Kara-Shahr</a> and at times <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hami">Hami</a>, controlling trade routes into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-alaq">Sultan Ahmad Khan</a> and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mansur-khan">Mansur Khan</a> they fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-empire">Ming</a> for Hami, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islamisation">Islamisation</a> of the Turpan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uyghurs">Uyghurs</a> was largely completed in this period, ending a long Buddhist tradition. The khanate was linked by marriage and rivalry to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/yarkent-khanate">Yarkent Khanate</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turfan">Turpan</a> khanate was weakened by divisions and absorbed into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/yarkent-khanate">Yarkent Khanate</a> in the 17th century. In 1680 the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/dzungar-khanate">Dzungar Khanate</a> conquered both, bringing the oases under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oirats">Oirat</a> rule.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/turpan-khanate.jpg" alt="Turpan Khanate" width="320" height="216"><figcaption>Image: Qiushufang · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1517_Yarkent_and_Turpan.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1462 CE – 1680 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>khanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Turpan</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Sultan Ahmad Khan, Mansur Khan, Shah Khan</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Chagatai Turkic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Eastern Moghulistan, Uyghurstan</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1571&amp;focus=turpan-khanate" aria-label="Turpan Khanate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6834 652 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Turpan Khanate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7154 908L6928 868M7154 908L7643 1242M7154 908L6921 1054M7154 908L7260 912"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6928 868h0M7643 1242h0M6921 1054h0M7260 912h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7154 908h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7154 908h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Turpan. <a href="/atlas?year=1571&amp;focus=turpan-khanate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
